'''Wuliwuli''' (also ''Wuli Wuli'', ''Wulli Wulli'') is an extinct [[Australian Aboriginal languages|Australian Aboriginal language]] of the [[Pama–Nyungan languages|Pama–Nyungan language family]] formerly spoken by the [[Wulli Wulli|Wulli Wulli people]] in [[Queensland]], [[Australia]].<ref name="AIATSIS" /> Wuliwuli is regarded as a dialect of [[Wakka Wakka language|Wakka Wakka]].<ref name=":dixon2002">{{cite book |last=Dixon |first=R. M. W. |url=http://www.cambridge.org/catalogue/catalogue.asp?isbn=0521473780 |title=Australian Languages: their nature and development |publisher=Cambridge University Press |year=2002 |author-link=R. M. W. Dixon}}</ref><ref>{{Cite journal |last=Hammarström |first=Harald |date=September 2015 |title=Ethnologue 16/17/18th editions: A comprehensive review: Online appendices |url=https://muse.jhu.edu/article/593411 |journal=Language |language=en |volume=91 |issue=3 |pages=s1–s188 |doi=10.1353/lan.2015.0049 |issn=1535-0665|hdl=11858/00-001M-0000-0029-1D58-0 |hdl-access=free }}</ref>

== Geographic distribution ==
The Wulli Wulli language region includes the landscape within the local government boundaries of the [[North Burnett Region|North Burnett Regional Council]], particularly the town of [[Eidsvold, Queensland|Eidsvold]] and the [[Burnett River|Auburn River catchment]], including the properties of [[Walloon, Queensland|Walloon]], [[Camboon, Queensland|Camboon]], and [[Hawkwood, Queensland|Hawkwood]].<ref name=":SLQ33">{{SLQ-CC-BY|url=https://maps.slq.qld.gov.au/iyil/view/33?embed=true|title=Wuli Wuli|publisher=[[State Library of Queensland]]|website=Queensland Aboriginal and Torres Strait Islander languages map|url-status=live|accessdate=6 June 2022}}</ref>

== Names ==
The alternate names for Wuliwuli are Wilili, Wililililee, Willillee, Wuli Wuli, Wulili, Wulilli, and Wulli Wulli.<ref>{{Cite web |title=Glottolog 5.1 - Wuliwuli |url=https://glottolog.org/resource/languoid/id/wuli1242 |access-date=2025-02-10 |website=glottolog.org}}</ref>

The name Wuliwuli is from the personal pronoun {{lang|wlu|wuli}}, meaning "they".<ref name=":0">{{Cite book |last=Holmer |first=Nils Magnus |url=https://archive.org/details/linguisticsurvey054dholm |title=Linguistic survey of south-eastern Queensland |date=1983 |publisher=Canberra : Dept. of Linguistics, Research School of Pacific Studies, Australian National University |others=Internet Archive |isbn=978-0-85883-295-4}}</ref>{{rp||page=38}}

== Phonology ==
The phonetic system of Wuliwuli is the same as that of [[Wakka Wakka language|Wakka Wakka]] and [[Barunggam language|Barunggan]].<ref name=":0" />{{rp||page=39}}

== Grammar ==
[[Word stem|Stem]] structure, the rules surrounding [[Morphological derivation|derivation]], and the derivative suffixes are largely the same as in [[Wakka Wakka language|Wakka Wakka]] and [[Barunggam language|Barunggan]].<ref name=":0" />{{rp||page=40}}

Locatives are expressed by the [[Ablative case|ablative]] [[affix]]. [[Allative case|Allatives]] are expressed either by the ablative, or the [[Adposition|postpositions]] {{lang|wlu|gu}} or {{lang|wlu|gua}}. [[Perlative case|Perlatives]] can also be found.<ref name=":0" />{{rp||page=41}}

=== Nouns ===
Examples of nominal derivational suffixes include {{lang|wlu|-gan}} ([[Grammatical gender|feminine]] suffix, e.g. {{lang|wlu|ɲuam}} "husband", {{lang|wlu|ɲuamgan}} "wife") and {{lang|wlu|-ŋal}} (sociative suffix, e.g. {{lang|wlu|bulu}} or {{lang|wlu|buluŋ}} "stomach, belly", {{lang|wlu|buluŋal}} "pregnant").<ref name=":0" />{{rp||page=40}}

The articles used are {{lang|wlu|-bam}} and {{lang|wlu|-ᶁin}}. Whether there is a difference in meaning between the two is unclear (e.g. {{lang|wlu|murunbam}} or {{lang|wlu|murunᶁin}} "(some) dark people"), although {{lang|wlu|-ᶁin}} may be used for relationship terms. On occasion both suffixes can be joined together (e.g. {{lang|wlu|ginbam}} or {{lang|wlu|ginbamᶁin}}, "(some) women").<ref name=":0" />{{rp||page=41}}

