=== Verbs ===
Examples of verbal derivational suffixes include {{lang|wlu|-ma-}} (causative, e.g. {{lang|wlu|garima}} "put in"), {{lang|wlu|-la-}} or {{lang|wlu|-le-}} (associative, e.g. {{lang|wlu|gaila}} "look for") and {{lang|wlu|-ŋi-}} (e.g. {{lang|wlu|gilaŋi}} "turn around").<ref name=":0" />{{rp||page=40}}

There are two regular patterns of conjugation, in addition to irregular ones. The first uses {{lang|wlu|-j-}} as the "thematic element"; the imperfective ends in {{lang|wlu|-ena}}, whereas the intentional ends in {{lang|wlu|-wa}}. The second uses a nasal ({{lang|wlu|-n-}}, {{lang|wlu|-ɲ-}}); the imperfective may end in {{lang|wlu|-na}}, the [[Perfective aspect|perfective]] in {{lang|wlu|-ɲi}}, and the intentional in {{lang|wlu|-wa}}.<ref name=":0" />{{rp||page=42}}

== Vocabulary ==
Some words from the Wulli Wulli language, as spelt and written by Wulli Wulli authors include:<ref name=":SLQ33" />

* {{lang|wlu|Ban}}: grass
* {{lang|wlu|Djigum}}: sun
* {{lang|wlu|Dungir}}: river
* {{lang|wlu|Gahr}}: echidna
* {{lang|wlu|Gamba dunba}}: good day
* {{lang|wlu|Goolah}}: koala
* {{lang|wlu|Gung}}: water
* {{lang|wlu|Gunyar}}: bird
* {{lang|wlu|Guraman}}: kangaroo
* {{lang|wlu|Guyu}}: fish
* {{lang|wlu|Moran}}: home/camp
* {{lang|wlu|Nyilung}}: land
* {{lang|wlu|Wangun}}: snake

